Command: USE [MOCK | "filename[sheet]"] [/FMT=format] [/NSCAN=n] [/MISSING=list]
     [/TYPES=collist]
Loads a dataset from CSV, ODF, or OOXML files into the Data Table.
USE MOCK generates synthetic test data.
Sheet selection (ODF/OOXML only):
  Append the sheet name in brackets inside the filename string.
  The name is case-sensitive and must match the tab label exactly.
  If omitted or not found, the first sheet is used.
  Example:  USE "sales.xlsx[Q2]"
Options:
  /FMT=format  Specifies the file format (CSV, ODF, OOXML).
               Default is auto-detected from file extension.
  /NSCAN=n     Number of rows to scan for type detection (default: 20).
  /MISSING="tok[,tok...]"  Literal strings treated as missing, in
               addition to the built-in "" and "." (never replaced).
               NUMERIC COLUMNS ONLY: in a character column ($ suffix,
               or settled to character by the scan) a matching field
               stays ordinary text, so declaring a sentinel for one
               column cannot discard a real string value elsewhere.
               Applies during the NSCAN-row scan too, so a declared
               token never forces a column to character and never
               triggers the "non-numeric value" warning -- it's
               expected, not an anomaly.  A quoted token may itself
               contain a comma, e.g. "NA,""a,b""".  CSV input only.
               Also legal per-dataset (in parentheses, like NSCAN=).
  /TYPES="col[,col...]"  Declare column types explicitly, opting those
               columns out of type detection.  Use when detection
               guesses wrong: pin the one awkward column instead of
               raising NSCAN for every column in the file.
               Two equivalent spellings:
                 /TYPES="AMOUNT,CODE$,QTY%"    (header suffixes)
                 /TYPES=(AMOUNT=NUM CODE=CHAR QTY=INT)
               $ = character, % = integer, no suffix = floating point.
               A value that cannot be represented in the declared type
               becomes missing, warned up to 10 times per file -- the
               same rule and message for CSV, ODF and OOXML alike.
               An unknown column name, or one declared twice, is an error.
               NOTE: declaring a column character RENAMES it (CODE ->
               CODE$), so a RENAME=/KEEP=/DROP= in the same USE must use
               the new name.  RENAME= ignores a name it cannot find
               rather than reporting it, so a stale reference there
               fails silently.  Run NAMES after USE to see the result.
               Also legal per-dataset (in parentheses, like NSCAN=).
Per-dataset options (in parentheses after a filename):
  (IF=expr)                Include a row from this input only when expr
                           is true, tested as the row comes off this
                           input (SAS WHERE=-style) -- against this
                           input's ORIGINAL column names, before this
                           spec's own RENAME=/KEEP=/DROP=/IN= and before
                           the row reaches any merge (/BY=, /INTERLEAVE,
                           /JOIN, /APPEND, positional). A non-matching row
                           behaves as if never present in that input.
  (KEEP=name ...)          Keep only the named columns.
  (DROP=name ...)          Drop the named columns.
  (RENAME=(old=new ...))   Rename columns (applied before KEEP/DROP);
                           all pairs apply against the original names.
                           If the new name's type suffix differs, the
                           column is retyped: float<->integer converts
                           values (float to integer truncates toward
                           zero); a numeric<->character change ($ added
                           or removed) is rejected and the whole RENAME
                           is aborted -- sdata does not parse a character
                           column's values as numbers (or format numeric
                           values as text) on rename, it only checks the
                           name suffix.
Merge (two or more comma-separated datasets):
  USE a, b                 Positional merge (combine rows by position).
  USE a, b /BY=var ...     Match merge (full outer; auto-sorted by BY).
  ... /INTERLEAVE          Interleave all rows in BY-sorted order.
  ... /JOIN                Cartesian inner join within each BY group.
  USE a, b /APPEND         Stack rows vertically (union of columns).
  IN=name marks a temporary 0/1 provenance variable, e.g.
    USE a(IN=ina), b(IN=inb) -- usable in LET/IF during the RUN that
    follows, auto-dropped afterward; KEEP it explicitly to make it a
    permanent variable instead.
Formula cells (ODF/OOXML):
  sdata has no built-in formula evaluator.
  If LibreOffice (soffice) is on PATH, it is invoked to recalculate
  formulas before import.  Otherwise cached values from the last save
  are used -- correct for normally-saved files, but potentially stale
  for volatile functions (TODAY, NOW, RAND) or manual-recalc files.
Execution: Declarative -- loads the dataset at once.
